<h1>About tuaccesible</h1>
<p class="lede">We're a digital accessibility consultancy. We audit web and mobile products against WCAG 2.2 and EN 301 549, train in-house teams, and stay with the fixes until the problem is actually solved.</p>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>Why an automated scan isn't enough</h2>
  <p>An automated tool crawls your site in seconds and returns a reassuring report. It finds the insufficient contrast and the image with no alternative text. Those are real findings and they matter.</p>
  <p>What it doesn't find is what actually breaks the experience:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The focus that vanishes when a menu opens.</li>
    <li>The form that flags an error in red without announcing it.</li>
    <li>The dropdown that opens and can't be closed from the keyboard.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Those are the barriers that lock someone out of your product. They're also the ones that end up in a complaint. And they surface by using the product, not by scanning it.</p>

  <h2>How we audit</h2>
  <p>We combine automated tools with a full manual review of the real product: keyboard navigation, screen readers, and zoom. The automated pass maps the ground. The work that finds the problems that matter is the manual one.</p>
  <p>The difference is who does that part. At tuaccesible it's María Pía, who has used a screen reader as her primary way of working for more than thirteen years. Operating the tool and using it every day are different things, and it shows in what gets tested and what gets missed.</p>

  <h2>No report ships on a single reading</h2>
  <p>Every audit and every workshop goes through a second review before it reaches you. One person audits, another checks the findings and the prioritization. It's slower, and that's deliberate: a criterion applied wrongly in a conformance report propagates into every remediation plan you build on top of it.</p>
  <p>tuaccesible grew out of a team that formed while working at the DALAT Foundation, and that habit of working in pairs comes from there.</p>
  <p>There are two of us. It is a small consultancy by choice, not by stage: what we sell cannot be subcontracted without losing exactly the thing that makes it different. That sets our capacity, and we would rather say so up front: if your deadline does not fit, we tell you in the first reply instead of taking the work and running late.</p>

  <h2>Recent work</h2>
  <p>A full audit of a web product and its browser extension, across web and Windows 11 environments. Manual evaluation with NVDA and keyboard-only navigation against WCAG 2.2 Level AA, EN 301 549, and the European Accessibility Act.</p>
  <p>The main finding was not a long list of different problems. It was the same one, repeated screen after screen. That changes the client's job completely: it is not a hundred fixes but one, applied to a component reused across the whole product. And it is why we prioritize by impact rather than by rule count — counting findings would have produced an alarming number and a wrong idea of the effort.</p>
  <p>The delivery included:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A 29-page accessibility review report, with findings prioritized by severity.</li>
    <li>Cognitive accessibility (COGA) recommendations.</li>
    <li>A draft accessibility statement in Spanish and English.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Two people from the team ran the audit. We publish a client's name only with their express permission; until we have it, we tell you about the work and not the name.</p>

  <h2>Who audits</h2>
  <p>Which tools we work with, from what experience, and with which certifications: María Pía covers that on <a href="/en/team/">Who audits your site</a>.</p>
